<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hydatius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400–c. 469</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hydatius was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquae-flaviae">Aquae Flaviae</a>, modern Chaves, in the Roman province of Gallaecia. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicle, continuing that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> to the year 468, is the main contemporary source for the Suebi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Alans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> after their invasion of 409. He records the collapse of Roman order in the west from the viewpoint of a provincial bishop.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the autumn of 409 the Vandals, Alans and Suebi, who had crossed the Rhine in 406, broke through the Pyrenees, and after two years of plunder they divided the Iberian provinces among themselves by lot in 411: according to the chronicler <b>Hydatius</b>, the Alans took Lusitania and Carthaginiensis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Very little is known of their administration, and <b>Hydatius</b> is the main source.</blockquote><a href="/state/alan-kingdom-in-hispania">Alan Kingdom in Hispania</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman rule faded, as recorded by <b>Hydatius</b>, bishop of Aquae Flaviae, and the name lived on in the medieval Kingdom of Galicia.</blockquote><a href="/state/gallaecia">Gallaecia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xinzo de Limia, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.06,-7.72&amp;year=444" aria-label="Xinzo de Limia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4362 666 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hydatius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4812 936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4812 936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.06,-7.72&amp;year=444">What was here in 444 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
